$\therefore \quad\left[\mathrm{Zn}\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)_{6}\right]^{2+}$ is an outer orbital complex and is diamagnetic.

On ionisation they give different ions

$\begin{array}{c}
{\left[\mathrm{Co}\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)_{4} \mathrm{Cl}_{2}\right] \mathrm{NO}_{2} \longrightarrow$}$ 
{\left[\mathrm{Co}\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)_{4} \mathrm{Cl}_{2}\right]^{+}+\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$} \\
{$\left[\mathrm{Co}\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)_{4} \mathrm{ClNO}_{2}\right] \mathrm{Cl} \longrightarrow$} 
{$\left[\mathrm{Co}\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)_{4} \mathrm{CINO}_{2}\right]^{+}+\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$}
\end{array}

So, they show ionisation isomerism.

The correct formula of the given complex is tetraammineaqua chlorocobalt (III) chloride is $\left[\mathrm{CoCl}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\right)\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)_{4}\right] \mathrm{Cl}_{2}$, because in it the oxidation number of Co is $+3$. While in rest other options $\mathrm{O}$. No. of Co is $+2 \begin{array}{l}
{\left[\mathrm{CoCl}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\right)\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)_{4}\right] \mathrm{Cl}_{2}$}$ \\
\Rightarrow x+(-1)+0+(0 \times4)+(-1) 2=0 \\
\Rightarrow x-3=0 \Rightarrow x=+3
\end{array}
